  urldate     = {2026-03-30},
  annote      = {Comment piece appearing in the Global Economy Opinion section. Chang argues that economics education at UK universities remains dominated by neoclassical orthodoxy despite growing real-world failures — ecological crises, inequality, financial instability — and student demands for reform following the 2007 crash. Drawing on a recent Rethinking Economics curriculum audit, he contends that top-ranked universities perform worst at preparing students for the real world, that pluralism (Keynesian, Marxist, institutionalist, etc.) has been displaced since the 1980s, and that genuine reform is a societal imperative rather than an academic distraction.},
